<Base title="Hypotheses"
      eyebrow="Everything unproved, set out together"
      dek="Every proposition this archive is currently entertaining but has not proved — each with what suggests it, what would settle it, and how much weight it deserves. Set out together so that none of them can quietly harden into fact.">
<section class="blk">

<div class="panel" style="margin:1.6rem 0">
    <p style="margin:0">
      A hypothesis is <strong>not</strong> a finding. Nothing on this page may be cited as though it were.
      When one is proved it moves to the page it belongs to and is marked
      <span class="chip doc">documented</span>; when it is disproved it stays here, marked as such, so that
      nobody spends a second afternoon on it. <a href={u("/method")}>Method &rarr;</a>
    </p>
  </div>
</section>
<section class="blk">
<h2>1. Where the surname comes from</h2>
  <p><strong>That <em>Lerena</em> is a Basque-formed house-name of the upper Ebro, not a reduction of Llerena in Badajoz.</strong></p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>For:</strong> Spain has exactly one real cluster of the name and it is <strong>La Rioja</strong>,
      at <strong>39.6&times;</strong> the national density; Andalusia and Madrid sit at 1.4&times;, which is
      merely what population size predicts. La Rioja lies on the upper Ebro, immediately south of the Basque
      provinces, where Basque house-names in <strong>-ena</strong> ("the house of") spill into Castilian
      country. <strong>Extremadura does not appear in the Spanish distribution at all.</strong></li>
    <li><strong>Against:</strong> <em>Llerena</em> has 36,828 bearers and <em>Ll-</em> does reduce to
      <em>L-</em> readily in Spanish-American registers.</li>
    <li><strong>To settle it:</strong> the La Rioja parish registers; and whether <em>Lerena</em> and
      <em>Llerena</em> ever alternate for one person in early River Plate records.</li>
  </ul>
  <div class="panel" style="border-left:3px solid var(--terra);margin:22px 0">
    <p class="lab" style="margin:0 0 8px">13 September 2026 &mdash; the test was met, and it answered the
      other way</p>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>The line above says what would settle it: <em>whether</em> Lerena <em>and</em> Llerena
        <em>ever alternate for one person in early River Plate records</em>. Following the 1866 Rosario
        act back through the Montevideo family produced a trunk, and at its root:</p>
      <p style="font-size:1.05rem"><strong>Sancho Llerena Aguilar</strong>, born
        <strong>Córdoba, Andalusia, 1767</strong>; a soldier; married at <strong>Canelones, Uruguay,
        8 January 1791</strong>; resident at Montevideo; died 1832. Great-grandfather of both the
        <strong>Lerena Lenguas</strong> and the <strong>Lerena Traibel</strong> &mdash; and so of the
        Luis Lerena Lenguas named in the Rosario act.</p>
      <p>Fourteen Uruguayan parish records are attached to him. They spell him <strong>Lerena</strong> in
        1793, 1840 and 1858; <strong>Llerena</strong> in 1804, 1811, 1827 and 1830; and once, in 1828,
        <strong><em>Sancho de Llerena</em></strong>.</p>
      <p><strong>That last form is the strongest item in the list.</strong> <em>De Llerena</em> is the
        toponymic construction &mdash; <em>of Llerena</em>, the Badajoz town &mdash; and a Basque
        house-name in <em>-ena</em> does not take <em>de</em>.</p>
      <p><strong>It does not overturn the distribution.</strong> La Rioja really is where the spelling
        clusters in Spain today, and that cluster has to be something. The honest synthesis is that
        <strong>both can be true at once</strong>: a Basque-formed <em>Lerena</em> of the upper Ebro,
        and separately a River Plate <em>Lerena</em> that is a reduction of Andalusian <em>Llerena</em>,
        carried over by a soldier from Córdoba and smoothed to one <em>l</em> by Uruguayan clerks within
        a generation. <strong>For this archive only the second matters</strong> &mdash; the question was
        never where the name is dense in Spain, but where <em>this family&rsquo;s</em> name came from.</p>
      <p class="cap">Tier: the trunk and the attachments are <strong>secondary</strong> &mdash;
        FamilySearch Tree is user-submitted, and nobody here has opened the fourteen acts to confirm they
        are one man. What is checkable by anyone who looks is the narrower fact: the Uruguayan parish
        registers spell this family <strong>both ways</strong>. And <strong>no Juan Carlos appears
        anywhere in that tree.</strong></p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <p><span class="chip inf">Split in two, 13 September &mdash; the River Plate half now points to Andalusia</span>
    <a href={u("/name")}>The name &rarr;</a></p>
</section>
<section class="blk">
<h2>1b. That the family was ITALIAN, not Spanish</h2>
  <p><strong>That Juan Carlos or María Lerena was an Italian immigrant to Rosario, and the family&rsquo;s
    name is nearer LERINA than Lerena.</strong></p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>For:</strong> a granddaughter&rsquo;s DNA carries <strong>0% Iberian</strong> and
      <strong>11.1% Italian</strong>, northern-dominant. Her other three grandparents are English and Cape
      Afrikaner, so the Italian has nowhere else to come from; at one quarter it implies roughly
      <strong>44% in him</strong> &mdash; about one Italian parent. <a href={u("/dna/")}>The DNA &rarr;</a></li>
    <li><strong>For:</strong> it explains the <strong>missing marriage</strong>. A couple who married in
      Italy before emigrating would never appear in the Rosario register, which has now been read clean
      for 1867&ndash;1885.</li>
    <li><strong>For:</strong> his death was registered <strong>LERINA</strong> &mdash; treated here for a
      month as a clerk&rsquo;s slip. CEMLA records <strong>Lerina as an <em>Italian</em> surname</strong>,
      arriving from Genova. The &ldquo;error&rdquo; may be the one document that got it right.</li>
    <li><strong>For:</strong> the register around his birth, read act by act, is <strong>full of Ligurian
      and Piedmontese names</strong>. Rosario in 1882 was the most Italian city in Argentina.</li>
    <li><strong>Against:</strong> ethnicity estimates are not records. At one quarter the noise is large,
      and MyHeritage is known to distribute Iberian across French, North Italian and Sardinian. And every
      indexed <em>Lerena</em> arrival at CEMLA is <em>Española</em>; it is the <em>Lerina</em> arrivals who
      are <em>Italiana</em>.</li>
    <li><strong>To settle it:</strong> a DNA match with a documented Italian or Argentine tree, and a
      sweep of the Rosario indexes under <strong>LERINA</strong>, which was never separately looked for.</li>
    <li><strong>Against, and it arrived within the hour:</strong> at low confidence <strong>all eight
      genetic groups</strong> are shown, and <strong>no Italian group is among the five that are
      legible</strong> &mdash; they are two South African, two British Isles, and the Río de la Plata one.
      The easiest confirmation was available and did not appear. Three group names remain unseen.</li>
    <li><strong>Against, 13 September, and this one is heavier:</strong> the Rosario baptism index has
      now been read end to end, 1731 to 1901, and the <em>one</em> Lerena in it is a <strong>Uruguayan
      from the Montevideo patriciate</strong> &mdash; a Lerena Lenguas married to a Juanic&oacute;, with
      an Oribe in the generation above. The surname in the River Plate has a documented home, and it is
      not Liguria. <a href={u("/searched/")}>What was read &rarr;</a></li>
    <li><strong>Still standing, and not dismissed:</strong> the death certificate says
      <strong>LERINA</strong>, and nothing found today explains that. The four LERINA arrivals of 1889
      remain excluded &mdash; but on their dates, not on a judgement about the surname, which was
      <a href={u("/corrections/")}>corrected the same day</a>.</li>
  </ul>
  <p><span class="chip inf">New, and weaker than when it was written &mdash; twice over</span>
    <a href={u("/dna/")}>The DNA &rarr;</a></p>
</section>
<section class="blk">
<h2>1c. That the family came from URUGUAY, not Argentina</h2>
  <p><strong>That Juan Carlos Lerena was a Uruguayan who came to Rosario, and that the Lerenas of the
    River Plate are a Montevideo house rather than a Santa Fe one.</strong></p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>For, and it is new:</strong> the Rosario baptism index has now been read end to end,
      <strong>both volumes, 1731 to 1901</strong>. The surname LERENA occurs <strong>once</strong> in a
      hundred and seventy years. The act behind that one entry was opened the same day, and it says of
      both parents: <em><strong>&ldquo;naturales del E. Oriental&rdquo;</strong></em> &mdash; natives of
      the Estado Oriental del Uruguay. <a href={u("/searched/")}>The sweep &rarr;</a></li>
    <li><strong>For:</strong> the mother in that act is a <strong>Juanicó</strong>, and the father a
      <strong>Lerena Lenguas</strong>. Both are Montevideo names, not Santa Fe ones.</li>
    <li><strong>For:</strong> the godfather &mdash; another <strong>Don Luis Lerena</strong> &mdash; did
      not attend. He was <strong>represented by proxy</strong>. Godparents stand by proxy when they are
      somewhere else, and for this family the somewhere else is across the river.</li>
    <li><strong>For, by explaining four separate negatives at once:</strong> no Rosario baptism for Pablo
      Armando; none for Juan Carlos; no Lerena marriage at Rosario, 1867&ndash;1885; and no Lerena in the
      parish index at all but for one Uruguayan. A family that lived in a parish leaves baptisms. This
      one leaves nothing &mdash; because it was not there.</li>
    <li><strong>Against:</strong> <strong>no link has been shown</strong> between Luis Lerena Lenguas and
      Juan Carlos Lerena. Two men sharing a surname in one city sixteen years apart is a lead, not a
      relation. The 1866 child is emphatically <em>not</em> Pablo Armando&rsquo;s sister.</li>
    <li><strong>Against:</strong> Pablo Armando himself was <strong>born at Rosario</strong> &mdash;
      documented twice over, on his passport and his enrolment card. Uruguayan origin for his father is
      not Uruguayan birth for him.</li>
    <li><strong>Where the DNA sits:</strong> awkwardly, and honestly so. The Río de la Plata genetic
      group that can only come from him <strong>spans both banks</strong>. It cannot tell Buenos Aires
      from Montevideo, and never could. What it does say is that the Argentine and the Uruguayan
      hypotheses are, genetically, <em>the same hypothesis</em>. <a href={u("/dna/")}>The DNA &rarr;</a></li>
    <li><strong>To settle it:</strong> <em>Uruguay, Registros Parroquiales, 1750&ndash;1930</em> &mdash;
      a baptism for <strong>Juan Carlos Lerena</strong> in the 1840s or 1850s, and a marriage to a
      <strong>María</strong> before 1882. Then Luis Lerena Lenguas and Julia Juanicó in Montevideo, whose
      own marriage would fix the generation above.</li>
  </ul>
  <div class="panel" style="border-left:3px solid var(--ochre);margin:22px 0">
    <p class="lab" style="margin:0 0 8px">The same evening &mdash; two routes meet</p>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>The act names the father <strong>Luis Lerena Lenguas</strong> and the proxy godfather
        <strong>Don Luis Lerena</strong>. That pair is enough to find the household in published
        Uruguayan genealogy: <strong>Luis Lerena y Fern&aacute;ndez (1811&ndash;1878)</strong>, widower
        of a <strong>Oribe</strong>, married secondly <strong>Justiniana Lenguas Gonz&aacute;lez</strong>,
        daughter of Gen. Pedro Lenguas. Their children carry the compound. The Montevideo patriciate.</p>
      <p>Two things follow. The <strong>proxy godfather gets a name</strong> &mdash; the child&rsquo;s
        paternal grandfather, in Montevideo &mdash; and the child&rsquo;s <strong>fifth given name is
        <em>Justiniana</em></strong>, for that grandmother. The act and the genealogy describe one
        family, and neither was written with the other in view.</p>
      <p>And <strong>one of his sons is already in this archive</strong>.
        <strong>Gilberto Justiniano Lerena Lenguas</strong>, Montevideo 1855 &ndash; 1912:
        <strong>co-founder of the Argentine Stud Book</strong>, breeder of the Quadruple Crown winner
        <em>Old Man</em>, the man for whom a Group 1 at Palermo was <strong>renamed in 1914</strong>. He
        has sat in this register since the 1895 Buenos Aires census, marked <em>high interest</em>, for a
        reason with nothing to do with Uruguay &mdash; <strong>Pablo Armando Lerena trained and owned
        racehorses</strong>.</p>
      <p>That was always the loudest coincidence in the file, and it was set aside <em>as</em> a
        coincidence. It still is one. But the parish register of Rosario and the turf of Buenos Aires now
        arrive at the same Montevideo house from opposite ends, by methods sharing no assumption.
        <a href={u("/horses/")}>The horses &rarr;</a></p>
      <p><strong>The evidence tiers matter here.</strong> The act is primary and was read at full
        resolution. Everything about the generations above it is <strong>secondary</strong> &mdash;
        Geneanet, Geni, a genealogical blog &mdash; none citing a checkable source. They agree with each
        other and with a document they were not written from. That is the best that can be said until the
        Montevideo registers are opened, and it is not proof.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="panel" style="margin:22px 0">
    <p class="lab" style="margin:0 0 8px">And a base rate, from evidence already on the shelf</p>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>Pablo Armando&rsquo;s enrolment card lives in army drawer <strong>&ldquo;Cajón 985
        (Lera)&rdquo;</strong>, which is filed <strong>by sound</strong> &mdash; so its Lerena section is
        a near-complete roll of Argentine Lerena men of enrolment age, <strong>each card naming his
        father and mother</strong>. It was read on 10 September to confirm that no card but his names a
        Juan Carlos and a María. It still doesn&rsquo;t. But the <em>fathers</em> were never followed up.
        Four of them trace:</p>
    </div>
    <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:14px">
      <table class="rec">
        <tr><th>The father on the cards</th><th>His wife</th><th>Where <em>he</em> was born</th></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Gilberto Lerena</strong></td><td>Julia Salvanach</td>
          <td><strong>Montevideo, 24 May 1855</strong> &mdash; Gilberto Justiniano Lerena Lenguas</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Alejandro M. Lerena</strong></td><td>Julia Bassani</td>
          <td><strong>Uruguay, 1839</strong> &mdash; and a son born at Montevideo, 1886</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Arturo Lerena</strong></td><td>Amalia Díaz</td>
          <td><strong>Montevideo, 1858</strong> &mdash; son of Avelino Lerena and Ambrosia Traibel</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Nemesio Ramón Lerena</strong></td><td>Laura Escobite</td>
          <td>married at <strong>Salto, Uruguay</strong>, 1903 &mdash; then Concordia, Entre Ríos, across
            the river</td></tr>
      </table>
    </div>
    <div class="prose" style="margin-top:14px">
      <p>Four branches, four different provinces of settlement &mdash; Entre Ríos, Mendoza, Misiones,
        Buenos Aires &mdash; and <strong>one common origin</strong>. Three more fathers could not be
        traced either way.</p>
      <p><strong>It is not a link</strong>; none of these men is Juan Carlos Lerena. It is a
        <strong>base rate</strong>: in this generation an Argentine Lerena is a Uruguayan family&rsquo;s
        son far more often than not &mdash; in every traceable case in the one drawer where they are
        gathered together. Juan Carlos Lerena, who left no trace whatever in the province where his son
        was born, fits that shape.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="panel" style="border-left:3px solid var(--accent);margin:22px 0">
    <p class="lab" style="margin:0 0 8px">13 September, later &mdash; the Montevideo household opened
      from the marriage to the last burial</p>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>Full text was given the phrase <strong>&ldquo;Lerena Lenguas&rdquo;</strong>. Seven hits in the
        whole corpus, all Montevideo, and between them they open the entire household.</p>
      <p><strong>San Francisco de Asís, Ciudad Vieja, act Nº 258</strong> &mdash; read from the image:</p>
      <p style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.05rem;color:var(--ink-2)">
        <em>En diez y nueve de Diciembre de mil ochocientos sesenta y cuatro &hellip; contrajo
        <strong>Don Luis Lerena y Lenguas</strong>, natural de esta República, soltero, é
        <strong>hijo legítimo de Don Luis y de Doña Justiniana Lenguas</strong>, con
        <strong>Doña Julia Juanicó</strong> &hellip; é hija legítima del <strong>Sr. Don Cándido</strong>
        y de <strong>Doña Juliana González</strong>.</em></p>
      <p><strong>That is the join the whole Montevideo argument rested on, and this morning it rested on
        a tree.</strong> Geneanet and the FamilySearch Tree both asserted this parentage and neither
        cited anything. The act states it. <strong>The generation above Luis Lerena Lenguas is no longer
        secondary.</strong></p>
      <p>It also names Julia Juanicó&rsquo;s parents for the first time &mdash; <strong>Dr Don Cándido
        Juanicó</strong>, who stood as a witness at his own daughter&rsquo;s wedding, and Doña Juliana
        González. And the date fits without strain: married 19 December 1864, first child born at
        Rosario 8 October 1865.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:14px">
      <table class="rec">
        <tr><th>The four children</th><th></th></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>María Julia Margarita Josefa Emirena Justiniana</strong></td>
          <td>b. <strong>Rosario</strong>, 8 Oct 1865 · m. Jaime Herrera, Montevideo, 1886</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Luis</strong></td><td>b. c.1872 · d. Montevideo, 12 Dec 1897, aged 25, unmarried</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Enrique</strong></td><td>b. Montevideo c.1873 · d. Montevideo, 16 May 1914, aged 41,
          unmarried, an <em>empleado</em></td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Ema Sixta</strong></td><td>b. 1876 · m. José Pedro Marsera, La Unión, Montevideo, 1913</td></tr>
      </table>
    </div>
    <div class="prose" style="margin-top:14px">
      <p><strong>Ema Sixta is the &ldquo;Emma Liseta, 1876, father Luis, mother Julia&rdquo;</strong>
        copied from a findmypast index on 9 September and left unremarked &mdash; four days before anyone
        knew whose daughter she was.</p>
      <p><strong>And Juan Carlos Lerena is not among them.</strong> The one Lerena family documented in
        the River Plate in this generation has been opened from the marriage to the last burial, and he
        is not in it. That costs this hypothesis nothing &mdash; it never claimed Pablo Armando descended
        from <em>this</em> household, only that the surname&rsquo;s River Plate home is Montevideo. What
        has changed is that the household is now fully mapped, so nobody need wonder about it again.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="note" style="margin-top:20px">
    <strong>And the negative, which is worth as much.</strong> The indexed Uruguayan record was searched
    the same evening. There is <strong>no Juan Carlos Lerena</strong> born in Uruguay between 1830 and
    1870 in <em>Uruguay, Catholic Church Records 1726&ndash;2000</em>, in <em>Uruguay, Civil Registration
    1879&ndash;2020</em>, or in the <em>Uruguay Baptisms 1750&ndash;1900</em> index; and <strong>no
    Lerena groom marrying a María</strong> in Uruguay between 1865 and 1885. Every <em>Juan Carlos
    Lerena</em> the index returns is a twentieth-century civil-registration card, decades too late.
    <br /><br />
    The record does not hand him over. What that narrows is <strong>which un-indexed pages are worth
    turning</strong>: the Montevideo parish registers, image by image, the way Rosario was read.
  </div>
  <p><span class="chip">Strong lead, no link proved &mdash; 13 September 2026</span>
    <a href={u("/searched/")}>What was read &rarr;</a></p>
</section>
<section class="blk">
<h2>2. How an Argentine came to be on a ship out of England</h2>
  <p><strong>That the River Plate reached the Cape <em>through Europe</em>, so he need only have changed ships at Southampton.</strong></p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>For:</strong> in the 1900s there was no dense direct Buenos Aires&ndash;Cape Town passenger
      service; sailing to Europe and transshipping was ordinary. Southampton was the hub of the mail run to
      the Cape. On this reading "he came from Argentina" and "they met on a ship from England" are not in
      conflict at all.</li>
    <li><strong>Also for:</strong> the South African War shipped roughly <strong>360,000 horses</strong>
      into the country, <strong>Argentina among the sources</strong>. He was 17 when it began and 20 when it
      ended, and he was a horseman. He was moving with a trade.</li>
    <li><strong>And for her:</strong> orphaned at 15 and 18, in the very years the Colonial Office and the
      emigration societies were running an organised scheme to send <strong>single British women to South
      Africa</strong>.</li>
    <li><strong>To settle it:</strong> <strong>UK outward passenger lists (BT 27)</strong>, indexed from
      1890. Note that she was <strong>Taylor until September 1911</strong> and they may have travelled
      separately.</li>
  </ul>
  <p><span class="chip lore">The meeting itself is family lore</span> <a href={u("/taylor")}>The Taylors &rarr;</a></p>
</section>
<section class="blk">
<h2>3. The consulship &mdash; and it may be the wrong country</h2>
  <p><strong>That the consular post, if it existed, was <em>Uruguayan</em> and not Argentine.</strong></p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>For:</strong> the family's own record carries an occupation entry
      <strong>"Uruguay consulate, Cape Town"</strong>, and a note that he was <em>"the ambassador for
      Argentina for a while — although <strong>Yvonne Lerena claims it was Uruguay</strong>."</em></li>
    <li><strong>Why it matters:</strong> this archive has searched the Argentine side and found nothing, and
      on the 1940 Argentine passport he is plainly the <em>bearer</em>, not the issuer. If the post was
      Uruguayan, both facts stop being puzzling at once.</li>
    <li><strong>And it fits the name.</strong> <a href={u("/name")}>Uruguay has the densest Lerena
      population on earth</a> — a small country where the name is common enough to make a resident Lerena a
      natural choice for an honorary post.</li>
    <li><strong>To settle it:</strong> the Uruguayan consulate at Cape Town — its officers, and the
      <em>exequatur</em> recognising them in the Union Government Gazette. A far narrower search than the
      Argentine one, and untried.</li>
  </ul>
  <p><span class="chip">Newly opened, and the better lead</span></p>
</section>
<section class="blk">
<h2>3b. Or the tradition grew out of the passport itself</h2>
  <p><strong>That "he was the Consul General" is a family memory of the family's Argentine consular passport.</strong></p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>For:</strong> the 1940 passport is a striking object, embossed, sealed and issued by the
      <em>Consulado General Argentino</em> at Cape Town — exactly the sort of heirloom a family remembers as
      a connection to the consulate. On the document itself he is plainly the <strong>bearer</strong>; the
      consular signature belongs to <strong>A. B. Bayne</strong>.</li>
    <li><strong>The alternative still open:</strong> an <strong>honorary</strong> consulship at some other
      date. Resident foreign nationals of standing were commonly appointed, and it would leave a trace in
      the host state's gazette but not in Argentine career foreign-service lists.</li>
    <li><strong>To settle it:</strong> the <em>exequatur</em> in the Union of South Africa Government
      Gazette; the Argentine <em>Boletín Oficial</em>; and the Cape Town consulate's staff lists across
      1900&ndash;1950 (look for A. B. Bayne to date the incumbency).</li>
  </ul>
  <div class="note" style="margin-top:20px">
    <strong>10 September 2026 &mdash; a date that gives the story a mechanism.</strong>
    Argentina and South Africa <strong>established diplomatic relations only on 10 September 1947</strong>.
    South Africa opened a consulate at Buenos Aires in 1938; Argentina opened its legation at Pretoria in
    1950. Before 1938 the published histories record nothing.
    <br /><br />
    Yet this family holds two Argentine documents proving a consular office at Cape Town in
    <strong>1908</strong> (the enrolment card&rsquo;s <em>&ldquo;Oficina enroladora de Capetown&rdquo;</em>)
    and in <strong>1940</strong> (the passport&rsquo;s <em>Oficina Consular Argentina</em>, with seal and
    a 16.50-peso consular stamp). <strong>Thirty-nine years before there were diplomatic relations, and
    thirty before the first consulate the histories mention.</strong>
    <br /><br />
    That is not a contradiction &mdash; consular relations are not diplomatic relations, and the usual
    instrument is the <strong>honorary consul</strong>: a resident merchant, unpaid, commissioned to stamp
    passports and register nationals. Such posts predate legations by decades and leave almost no trace.
    <em>A. B. Bayne</em> is not a Spanish name.
    <br /><br />
    <strong>So the tradition is not proved, but it is now explicable</strong> &mdash; which is a different
    and more honest thing. There <em>was</em> an Argentine consular office at the Cape, it ran for at least
    thirty-two years, and it was almost certainly honorary and locally staffed. In a city with a handful
    of Argentine nationals, the man who held the country&rsquo;s passport and dealt with its consulate is
    exactly the man a family remembers as &ldquo;something with the consulate&rdquo;.
  </div>
  <p style="margin-top:18px"><span class="chip inf">The claim as told is disputed</span>
    <a href={u("/passport")}>The passport &rarr;</a></p>
</section>
<section class="blk">
<h2>4. The ostrich farm</h2>
  <p><strong>That there was no ostrich farm, and the tradition has attached itself to the wrong man or the wrong country.</strong></p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>For:</strong> a sweep of South African deeds registers finds him holding
      <strong>no land at all</strong>. Every document places him in Cape Town racing — stables at Rugby, a
      training yard, "race horse owner" at death, "horse-dealer" at marriage.</li>
    <li><strong>Against dismissing it:</strong> deeds coverage is partial; a farm could have been
      <strong>leased</strong>, leaving no transfer; and <strong>Oudtshoorn's own records have not been
      searched</strong>. Ostriches were also farmed in Argentina's near relative, the rhea — a tradition
      could have crossed with him.</li>
    <li><strong>To settle it:</strong> Oudtshoorn and Little Karoo farm and lease records; the C.P. Nel
      Museum; the Cape deeds registry under both <em>Lerena</em> and <em>Lerina</em>.</li>
  </ul>
  <p><span class="chip inf">Doubtful, not disproved</span> <a href={u("/south-africa")}>South Africa &rarr;</a></p>
</section>
<section class="blk">
<h2>5. Where Pablo Armando's land was</h2>
  <p><strong>That he held land at Blouberg, which the deeds sweep failed to find.</strong></p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>For:</strong> the family record states plainly that <em>"he owned a lot of land up around
      the <strong>Blouberg</strong> area"</em> — the coast north of Cape Town, beyond Milnerton and Rugby,
      where his stables were.</li>
    <li><strong>Against:</strong> a search of the South African deeds registers returned
      <strong>no land at all</strong> in his name.</li>
    <li><strong>Most likely explanation:</strong> the search, not the claim, is at fault. Deeds indexing is
      partial and his surname is written <em>Lerena</em>, <em>Lerina</em> and <em>Larena</em> across
      different records.</li>
    <li><strong>To settle it:</strong> the Cape deeds registry for the Blouberg and Milnerton areas, under
      every spelling.</li>
  </ul>
  <p><span class="chip">Open — and it bears on the ostrich farm above</span></p>
</section>
<section class="blk">
<h2>6. That he left Argentina as a political refugee</h2>
  <div class="note" style="margin:16px 0"><strong>10 September 2026 — answered from Amsterdam, and it
    goes against the story.</strong> The <strong>International Institute of Social History</strong>, the
    principal repository for the international left, searched its OCR&rsquo;d corpus of Latin American
    anarchist and labour periodicals, 1880&ndash;1940: <em>&ldquo;nothing popped up.&rdquo;</em> Re-run
    here the same day and the null holds &mdash; the 171 apparent &ldquo;Lerena&rdquo; hits are the
    <em>Conde de Lerena</em> of the 1787 Spanish census and <em>Osimani y Llerena</em>, a street in
    Montevideo where an anarchist weekly had its office; the 16 &ldquo;Lerina&rdquo; hits are OCR breakage
    of <em>ballerina</em>.
    <br /><br />
    <strong>But that is the weakest of the three objections, and it should not be dressed up.</strong> A
    horse-dealer in his twenties would not be in the anarchist press unless he were notable, and most
    people never appear in any periodical. <strong>The real objection is the enrolment card:</strong> in
    1908 he walked into his own government&rsquo;s consular office at Cape Town and wrote down his name,
    his conscript class, his birthplace and both his parents. A man hiding from the Argentine state does
    not do that. And <strong>Ley 4144 deported foreigners, not nationals</strong> &mdash; the mechanism
    the story implies could not have been used on him.
    <br /><br />
    <strong>Not disproved. Now unlikely.</strong> The AGN expulsion files remain the only place that could
    settle it positively.</div>
  <p><strong>That Pablo Armando fled Argentina because he was in political trouble on the left.</strong></p>
  <p class="cap">Family testimony, carried three generations and repeated to David by his mother, Cheryl
    Anne Lerena, in September 2026: that he &ldquo;ran away from Argentina as he was a communist on the
    run.&rdquo; Never proven.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Why it deserves better than folklore:</strong> it is the <em>only</em> story the family
      carries that explains a <em>departure</em>. Rosario, the horses, the consulship, the ostriches — all
      describe what he was. This alone says why he left, and a reason to leave is precisely what the
      documentary record has refused to supply.</li>
    <li><strong>The correction the story needs:</strong> the Argentine Communist Party did not exist until
      <strong>1918</strong>. He left around 1903–05. The radical movement in Argentina then was
      overwhelmingly <strong>anarchist and anarcho-syndicalist</strong>. Families routinely retrofit
      &ldquo;communist&rdquo; onto earlier left politics once that becomes the available word — so the
      claim to test is &ldquo;in political trouble on the left,&rdquo; not the literal one.</li>
    <li><strong>The mechanism that would make it true:</strong> <strong>Ley 4144, the <em>Ley de
      Residencia</em></strong> of November 1902, which let the government deport any foreign-born person
      deemed a threat <strong>without trial or appeal</strong>. It was used hard against anarchist
      organisers in exactly 1903–05. And <strong>Rosario</strong> — great river port, rail head, big dock
      and railway workforce — was among the most militant anarchist cities in the country.</li>
    <li><strong>The chronology fits tightly.</strong> Born February 1882; his son Roque born at Cape Town
      in November 1905. He left Argentina somewhere in <strong>1903–05</strong>, aged 21 to 23.</li>
    <li><strong>*** And here is the part that may matter most. ***</strong> The Ley de Residencia applied to
      <strong>foreigners</strong>. An Argentine national could be prosecuted but not deported under it. So
      if he was <em>expelled</em>, <strong>he was not Argentine-born</strong> — and our failure to find him
      in the Rosario baptism register stops being a puzzle and becomes a consequence. That would sit with
      the Uruguayan density of the surname, the Uruguayan-born Lerenas in the 1895 census, and the family
      note that the consulship may have been Uruguayan.</li>
    <li><strong>Against:</strong> nothing documentary supports it yet, and he was issued an Argentine
      passport in 1940 — so he was neither stateless nor permanently barred. If he merely fled prosecution
      rather than being deported, he could be Argentine-born after all.</li>
    <li><strong>To settle it:</strong> Ministerio del Interior expulsion files at the Archivo General de la
      Nación; Santa Fe provincial and Rosario police records 1902–05; the anarchist press, chiefly
      <em>La Protesta</em>, much of it held at the International Institute of Social History in Amsterdam.
      None has been asked.</li>
  </ul>
  <p><span class="chip lore">Family lore — untested, falsifiable, and pointing at named archives</span></p>
</section>
<section class="blk">
<h2>7. The Llorens baptism</h2>
  <p><strong>That Pablo Armando descends from the Roberto Llorens and Francisca Fernández named in a San Miguel baptism of 1872.</strong></p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Why it was ever entertained:</strong> the child baptised there was
      <strong>Roberto Juan Roque</strong>, and both <em>Roberto</em> and <em>Roque</em> recur in this
      family — a <strong>Roberto Juan Roque Lerena</strong> was born in 1944.</li>
    <li><strong>Against:</strong> the surname is <strong>Llorens</strong>, a Catalan name meaning Lawrence,
      not a variant of Lerena; and the streets and parish point to <strong>Buenos Aires</strong>, not
      Rosario. <strong>David Defranceski states plainly that these records are unproven</strong>, and this
      archive treats them so.</li>
    <li><strong>To settle it:</strong> Pablo Armando's own baptism at Rosario, spring 1882, which would name
      his parents outright and end the question.</li>
    <li><strong>What happened when it was tested:</strong> the Rosario index has now been read across
      <strong>all twenty-six letters for 1882</strong>, and there is <strong>no Pablo Armando under any
      surname</strong> — the F section, where a Fernández registration would sit, is clear. That does not
      make the Llorens baptism false; it removes the one route that could have connected it to him.</li>
  </ul>
  <p><span class="chip inf">Unproven; not cited elsewhere on this site</span></p>
</section>
<section class="blk">
<h2>8. A Carmen in the Argentine line</h2>
  <p><strong>That the name <em>Carmen</em>, surviving into 1958, descends from Pablo Armando's own mother or grandmother.</strong></p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Against:</strong> <em>Carmen</em> is among the commonest female names in the
      Spanish-speaking world — a devotional name from <em>Nuestra Señora del Carmen</em>. This family was
      Catholic; a devotional choice needs no ancestor to explain it.</li>
    <li><strong>For:</strong> look at what the family actually used. The <em>male</em> Spanish names are
      abundant and persistent across three South African generations — Pablo Armando, Roque Luis Armando,
      Ricardo Juan Carlos, Nuno Fernando, Roberto Juan Roque, Anton Armando, Juan Carlos, Gilberto. The
      <em>female</em> ones are scarce, and <strong>Carmen</strong> is the clearest survival among them.</li>
    <li><strong>And the grandmothers do not account for it.</strong> Patricia Carmen Lerena's grandmothers
      were <a href={u("/taylor")}>Mary Septima Taylor</a>, an Englishwoman of Devon, and the mother of
      Catherine Mary Sophia Booyzen, an Afrikaner. Neither was a Carmen. Spanish custom names children for
      grandparents; if this name descends rather than being chosen fresh, it descends from
      <strong>further back on the Lerena side</strong>.</li>
    <li><strong>Use:</strong> weak as proof, useful as a filter. In Argentine and Uruguayan Lerena records,
      <strong>give extra weight to any household containing a Carmen</strong>.</li>
  </ul>
  <p><span class="chip">Inference from naming — modest, and worth carrying</span></p>
</section>
<section class="blk">
<h2>9. The remaining smaller ones</h2>
  <div class="scroll">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th style="width:20rem">Hypothesis</th><th>Standing</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><strong>The 1879 twin was a girl</strong></td>
          <td>Required by the arithmetic of "Septima": Mary Septima is only the seventh daughter if the twin
            was female. <span class="chip">Sound but formally unproved</span></td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>The family was regularised in the church in 1911</strong></td>
          <td>Son baptised 12 June 1911; parents married 3 September 1911, both bachelor and spinster; the
            marriage examined by a "Bert…" who is very likely Bertram W. Glynn, the priest who performed the
            baptism. <span class="chip">Strong</span></td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Gavin Lerena's descent</strong></td>
          <td>Pablo Armando &rarr; Ricardo &rarr; Carlos "Tex" &rarr; Gavin, per the family chart, and the
            racing press independently describes the same succession of trainers.
            <span class="chip">Family chart, not independently verified</span></td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>He imported horses for the government</strong></td>
          <td>The family record states he <em>"used to import horses into South Africa from Argentina for
            the ZA government"</em>, and separately for his own stables. This is precisely the testable form
            of the horse claim that this archive set out on day one.
            <span class="chip lore">Family testimony; find the contracts</span></td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>He left Argentina for a political reason</strong></td>
          <td>The family record says he <em>"ran from Argentina for a communism related reason"</em> and
            flags it as unsubstantiated. He was Argentine <em>by birth</em> and served in the National
            Guard, so this would place his departure after 1915.
            <span class="chip lore">Unsubstantiated</span></td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>The family concealed the illegitimacy</strong></td>
          <td>The family record notes that on a <strong>copy document of 1916</strong> the marriage
            <em>"dates have been manipulated to show an earlier year… but it was 1911"</em> — which would
            place the wedding before Roque's 1905 birth.
            <span class="chip">Strong, and the 1916 copy is worth finding</span></td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Two men called "Bob"</strong></td>
          <td>The family record gives the nickname <em>Bob</em> to <strong>both</strong> Pablo Armando and
            his son Ricardo. The Sporting Post's "Bob Lerena, grandfather of Spike" must still be Pablo
            Armando, since Ricardo was Spike's <em>father</em> — but the ambiguity is a live hazard in
            racing records. <span class="chip inf">A trap to watch for</span></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
</section>
<section class="blk">
<h2>Retired</h2>
  <p>
    <span class="chip doc">Proved</span> <strong>That Mary Septima Taylor was born in England.</strong>
    This archive doubted it for part of one day, on the strength of a badly filtered search that returned no
    English record. The family's own sourced tree carries the answer: <strong>born at Bideford, Devon,
    17 September 1884</strong>, cited to the England and Wales birth registration index and the GRO at
    Southport, and christened at St Margaret's, Northam. The doubt was mine and it was wrong.
    <a href={u("/taylor")}>The Taylors &rarr;</a>
  </p>
  <p>
    <span class="chip doc">Proved</span> <strong>That "Rosalina Wilhelmina Chappell" was a previous married
    name.</strong> This archive proposed it as one of two readings. The family record settles it: Roque
    married <strong>Rose Forbes, who was Doreen Chappell's mother</strong> — so Chappell was Rose's name by
    her first marriage, and Roque married his own brother's mother-in-law.
    <a href={u("/horses")}>The horses &rarr;</a>
  </p>
  <p>
    <span class="chip inf">Disproved</span> <strong>That Pablo Armando Lerena and Robert Paul Lerena were
    brothers.</strong> This archive proposed it for one day, on the ground that the names and ages would not
    reconcile. The Corpus Christi baptism register named <strong>Pablo Armando</strong> as the father of the
    son whose death notice names <strong>Roberto</strong> — one man, two forms of his name. The inference is
    withdrawn. <a href={u("/passport")}>The passport &rarr;</a>
  </p>
  <p>
    <span class="chip inf">Disproved</span> <strong>That the 61 South African Lerenas are one kindred.</strong>
    At least two unconnected populations bear the name there. <a href={u("/south-africa")}>South Africa &rarr;</a>
  </p>
</section>
